REA — Recreate Extractable Field → Auto-Fill

Recreate the Extractable Field feature from scratch. Do NOT revert commits or restore the previous implementation.

First inspect the existing template parameter/configuration, persistence, rendering, and auto-fill architecture so the new implementation integrates cleanly with the current REA system.

CORE CONCEPT

An existing required template parameter can be marked as Extractable.

For example, a message template may contain:

{reference_number}
{date}
{time}

The {reference_number} field normally receives a value such as:

MP260831.1923.T7382831

The administrator should be able to configure {reference_number} as an Extractable Field.

STEP 1 — DEFINE THE EXAMPLE INPUT

On the Admin Dashboard, when configuring {reference_number} as Extractable, the admin provides an example of the value that this field will receive:

MP260831.1923.T7382831

The example input is then used to define what portions of the value should be extracted.

STEP 2 — DEFINE EXTRACTIONS USING SQUARE BRACKETS

The admin should be able to wrap the portions they want to extract in square brackets.

For example:

MP[260831].[1923].T7382831

This means:

Extraction 1 = 260831
Extraction 2 = 1923

The square brackets are configuration syntax only. They must NOT become part of the actual extracted value.

REA should automatically detect the bracketed portions and present them as separate extracted values.

STEP 3 — MAP EXTRACTED VALUES TO EXISTING TEMPLATE FIELDS

Each extracted value must be independently mapped to another existing template parameter.

For example:

[260831] → {date}

[1923] → {time}

The admin should be able to select the destination field from the existing fields/parameters of the template.

The extracted values are therefore not merely stored as named data. Their primary purpose is to provide values to other existing template fields.

STEP 4 — OPTIONAL TRANSFORMATION / MODIFICATION

Before an extracted value is placed into its target field, the admin must be able to configure an optional transformation/modification.

For example:

Extracted value:

260831

Target:

{date}

Transformation:

YYMMDD → YYYY-MM-DD

Result:

2026-08-31

Therefore:

260831 → 2026-08-31 → {date}

Similarly:

Extracted value:

1923

Target:

{time}

Transformation:

HHmm → HH:mm

Result:

19:23

Therefore:

1923 → 19:23 → {time}

The transformation step must occur AFTER extraction and BEFORE the value is assigned to the target field.

The overall pipeline should therefore be:

Source Field
→ Extraction
→ Mapping
→ Transformation
→ Target Field

For example:

{reference_number}
→ [260831]
→ {date}
→ YYMMDD → YYYY-MM-DD
→ 2026-08-31

and:

{reference_number}
→ [1923]
→ {time}
→ HHmm → HH:mm
→ 19:23

ADMIN CONFIGURATION UI

Structure the configuration into three clear stages:

1. DEFINE EXTRACTION

Source field:
{reference_number}

Example input:
MP260831.1923.T7382831

Extraction definition:
MP[260831].[1923].T7382831

Detected extractions:
- 260831
- 1923

2. MAP EXTRACTED VALUES

Extraction 1:
260831

Auto-fill target:
{date}

Extraction 2:
1923

Auto-fill target:
{time}

3. CONFIGURE TRANSFORMATION

For each mapping, optionally configure how the extracted value should be modified before being assigned to the target field.

Example:

260831
→ Date transformation
→ 2026-08-31
→ {date}

1923
→ Time transformation
→ 19:23
→ {time}

Provide sensible previews wherever practical so the administrator can see the result of the configured transformation.

RUNTIME BEHAVIOUR

Once configured, the agent should NOT need to manually enter {date} and {time}.

The agent simply enters:

MP260831.1923.T7382831

into:

{reference_number}

REA should then:

1. Detect the configured extraction rule.
2. Extract 260831 and 1923.
3. Apply their configured transformations.
4. Auto-fill {date} and {time}.
5. Leave the agent with the resulting populated fields.

IMPORTANT ARCHITECTURAL REQUIREMENTS

Keep these concerns separate:

Source Field
→ Extraction
→ Mapping
→ Transformation
→ Target Field

Do not tightly couple extraction logic to specific target fields.

The same extracted value mechanism should eventually be reusable for other fields such as transaction IDs, amounts, account numbers, phone numbers, dates, times, etc.

The square-bracket syntax should be a simple administrator-friendly extraction-definition mechanism. Do not require administrators to write regular expressions.

Do not unnecessarily expose regex implementation details in the normal Admin UI.

Handle invalid configurations such as:
- unmatched brackets
- empty brackets
- nested brackets
- invalid extraction definitions

with clear validation messages.

DATE AND TIME

Pay particular attention to Date Only and Time Only target controls.

Ensure transformed values are actually compatible with the target control's expected value format.

For example:

Date Only:
2026-08-31

Time Only:
19:23

Do not allow the extraction/auto-fill mechanism to bypass or break the existing control-type behavior.

VISIBILITY

Keep auto-fill completely separate from field visibility.

If a target field is configured as "Not always visible", extracting and auto-filling its value must NOT automatically force it to become visible unless the existing visibility rules explicitly require it.

Do not use auto-fill as an implicit visibility mechanism.

PERSISTENCE

All extractable-field configuration must persist correctly when the template is saved and later reloaded.

The following must survive persistence:

- Extractable field setting
- Example input
- Extraction definitions
- Multiple extracted values
- Mapping to target fields
- Transformation configuration
- Any required metadata

MULTI-TENANCY AND EXISTING BEHAVIOUR

Preserve REA's existing multi-tenant/company isolation.

Do not introduce cross-company access.

Preserve the existing agent profile model.

Ordinary agents must NOT be given new PIN requirements or authentication requirements simply to use this feature.

Admin-only configuration should continue to follow the existing admin authorization model.

TESTING

Add regression/unit/integration tests covering at minimum:

1. A field can be marked Extractable.
2. Example input can be configured.
3. Square brackets correctly identify one extracted substring.
4. Multiple bracketed substrings are detected independently.
5. Brackets are removed from the actual extracted values.
6. Extracted values can be mapped to existing template fields.
7. Normal text auto-fill works.
8. Date transformation works.
9. Time transformation works.
10. Date Only receives the correct value format.
11. Time Only receives the correct value format.
12. Multiple extracted values can populate multiple fields.
13. Visibility rules are not overridden by auto-fill.
14. Configuration survives save/reload.
15. Invalid bracket configurations are rejected clearly.
16. Multi-tenant isolation is preserved.

IMPLEMENTATION APPROACH

Do not rewrite unrelated parts of REA.

Do not simply patch the old Extractable Field implementation without understanding it.

First inspect the current code and identify:
- Where template parameters are defined.
- How template configurations are persisted.
- How field values are stored at runtime.
- How auto-fill currently works.
- How control types such as Date Only and Time Only are represented.
- How field visibility is implemented.
- Which parts of the previous Extractable Field implementation can safely be removed or replaced.

Then implement the new feature using the smallest clean architecture that fits the existing system.

Avoid hardcoding specific template names, field names, or the MP transaction format. The example:

MP[260831].[1923].T7382831

is only an example of how the feature should work.

At the end, report:

1. The existing architecture you found.
2. The files/components changed.
3. The new extraction → mapping → transformation → auto-fill flow.
4. Any old Extractable Field code that was replaced or removed.
5. Tests added and their results.
6. Any limitations or decisions that should be addressed later.

Do not revert commits. Do not modify unrelated functionality. Build this as a clean recreation of the Extractable Field feature around the existing REA template system.

DO NOT PUT THE SMART EXTRACTOR HEADER OR FEATURE HERE (IF IT IS ALREADY THERE, REMOVE IT)